//! [module-table]
The following table summarizes the key \l{Qt Core}{Qt Core module} classes for managing
application configuration. Utilize \c{QSettings} to persist user preferences,
or \c{QCommandLineParser} to process command-line arguments at startup.

\table 80%
  \header
    \li Class
    \li Purpose
    \li Notes
  \row
    \li \c{QSettings}
    \li Stores and retrieves application settings across sessions.
    \li On \macos, settings are stored in the user domain by default. On Linux,
        an \c{.ini} file is written to \c{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}. Pass
        \c{QSettings::IniFormat} to force the INI format on all platforms.
  \row
    \li \c{QCommandLineParser}
    \li Parses command-line arguments and generates \c{--help} output automatically.
    \li Add options with \c{addOption()} and retrieve values with \c{value()}.
        Call \c{process()} to apply the parsed arguments to the \l{QApplication}{application}.
  \row
    \li \c{QProcess}
    \li Starts external processes and communicates with them through standard I/O.
    \li Use \c{start()} to launch the process. The \c{finished()} signal is emitted
        when the process is terminated. The working directory is set with
        \c{setWorkingDirectory()} before the process is started.
  \row
    \li \c{QFileSystemWatcher}
    \li Monitors the file system for changes to files and directories.
    \li Watched paths are added with \c{addPath()}. The \c{fileChanged()} and
        \c{directoryChanged()} signals are emitted when the corresponding path
        is modified by the operating system.
\endtable

\note On \macos and Linux, file system event delivery is handled by the OS kernel.
The \c{QFileSystemWatcher} implementation is selected at build time.
//! [module-table]

